Refractive errors
Opthalmology & ENT
Painless blurred vision, Correctable with pinhole, Chronic eye strain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-038
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe frontal headache and generalized eye strain causing extreme distress and inability to complete critical work tasks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient suffering from an acute, severe, intractable frontal headache and markedly blurred vision following prolonged visual tasks. Urgent physical assessment was required to definitively rule out acute angle-closure glaucoma, giant cell arteritis, or an acute intracranial mass lesion, and to differentiate these emergencies from severe asthenopia secondary to uncorrected refractive error, ensuring safety prior to morning general practice hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is pounding right behind my eyes, my vision is completely blurred, and I've been straining so hard to read my screen that I feel nauseous."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive, painless, and bilateral blurring of vision over the last several months, previously ignored. Tonight, after 10 hours of uninterrupted, demanding visual tasks (reading small print on a monitor), the patient developed a severe frontal and periorbital headache. Endorses chronic generalized eye strain, fatigue, and pronounced asthenopia. Reports an inability to clearly resolve distant objects while near vision remains intact (suspected myopia), and relies on involuntary habitual squinting to mechanically improve visual clarity. Denies jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, or seeing halos around lights.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, holding head, unable to continue working.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Squinting heavily, rubbing the forehead and temples, appearing fatigued.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity is significantly reduced bilaterally (6/24) for distance. However, there is an immediate, transient, and striking improvement of visual acuity back to 6/6 when looking directly through a physical pinhole occluder. Pupils are equal, round, and briskly reactive to light and accommodation. No relative afferent pupillary defect. Extraocular movements are full and intact. Corneas are perfectly clear, and the anterior chambers are deep without cells or flare. Fundoscopy is entirely normal with sharp optic discs. Neurological exam reveals no focal deficits. Palpation of temporal arteries yields no tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Poorly lit home office; dual monitors set to extreme brightness.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncorrected Refractive Error (Myopia) with Severe Asthenopia - Status: Acute symptomatic presentation
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Deep anterior chambers, clear corneas (no edema), normal reactive pupils (not mid-dilated and fixed), and the dramatic improvement in vision using a simple pinhole occluder.

Giant Cell Arteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's young age, lack of temporal artery tenderness, absence of jaw claudication, and the completely reversible nature of the visual blur with a pinhole.

Intracranial Hypertension (e.g., mass lesion) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely normal, sharp optic discs on fundoscopy (no papilledema), normal neurological exam, and symptoms clearly tied to prolonged visual exertion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe frontal headache and blurred vision, symptoms that classically overlap with serious neuro-ophthalmological emergencies. Urgent, direct clinical evaluation was categorically required to rule out acute glaucoma or temporal arteritis. The critical diagnostic maneuver was the use of a pinhole occluder, which instantly and completely resolved the visual deficit. This objective finding decisively localized the pathology to a simple optical focusing problem (refractive error) driving severe muscular asthenopia (ciliary spasm/eye strain). By ruling out catastrophic intracranial or ocular pathology, the urgent visit prevented an unnecessary emergency department workup and provided immediate reassurance and symptomatic relief strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Paracetamol 1g and Ibuprofen 400mg STAT for the severe muscular tension headache. Directed the patient to immediately cease all visual tasks for the remainder of the night. Generated a routine referral to an optometrist for formal manifest clinical refraction and prescription lenses.
Positive Behavioral Change: To break the cycle of ciliary muscle spasm during screen work, the patient must strictly implement the 20-20-20 rule using a physical, loud kitchen timer placed across the room. Every 20 minutes, the timer forces the patient to stand up, walk over, and look at an object 20 feet away for 20 seconds. The immediate release of the muscular tension in the eye provides direct positive reinforcement, embedding the habit of visual breaks to prevent future asthenopic headaches.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Must book an optometry appointment within the next 7 days for definitive optical correction.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the headache becomes explosive and worst-of-life, if vomiting occurs, if vision suddenly goes black and does not improve with squinting, or if weakness develops in any limb.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a physical focusing error of the eye; requires an optometrist to prescribe the exact corrective lenses.
Clinical Course: The acute headache and eye strain will resolve completely by tomorrow morning following rest. The blurred vision will persist until you receive corrective glasses.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your headache suddenly becomes the worst you have ever felt in your life, or if you develop numbness or weakness anywhere in your body, call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your eyes are physically the wrong shape to focus light perfectly on the retina. You have been unconsciously clenching the tiny muscles inside your eyes all day to try and force them into focus, which has caused a massive tension headache (asthenopia). You simply need glasses.
Actionable Steps: Stop all screen work immediately for tonight. Take the painkillers and go to sleep. You must see an optometrist this week to get prescription glasses to permanently fix the focusing error.

